@page "/getting-started"

<PageTitle>Getting started · BbScanner</PageTitle>

<div class="bb-intro">
    <h1>Getting started</h1>
    <p class="bb-subtitle">
        One package and one namespace - there are no services to register, no script tag and no stylesheet
        link. Put the scanner on an interactive page - WebAssembly, Server, Auto or Hybrid - and it opens the
        camera by itself; everything after the camera hands over its pixels is managed code.
    </p>
</div>

<div class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-primary"></span> Install</h3>
    <p class="bb-card-desc">
        In a Blazor Web App, add the package to the project the scanning pages render in: the server project
        for <code>InteractiveServer</code> pages, the <code>.Client</code> project for
        <code>InteractiveWebAssembly</code> or <code>InteractiveAuto</code> ones, whose assembly has to be
        downloaded to the browser.
    </p>
    <pre class="bb-code">dotnet add package Bit.BbScanner</pre>
    <p class="bb-card-desc">Then add the namespace to <code>_Imports.razor</code>:</p>
    <pre class="bb-code"><span class="k">@@using</span> Bit.BbScanner</pre>
    <p class="bb-card-desc">
        That is the whole setup. The package's Blazor initializer adds
        <code>_content/Bit.BbScanner/bit-bbscanner.css</code> to the page, and the component imports its own
        JavaScript module the first time it renders. Linking the stylesheet yourself also works - the
        injection then stands down.
    </p>
</div>

<div class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-success"></span> A first scanner</h3>
    <p class="bb-card-desc">
        The camera starts as soon as the component is ready (<code>AutoStart</code> is on by default) and the
        browser asks for permission the first time. The scanner prerenders safely on the server and starts once
        the page is interactive. Any interactive render mode will do - <code>InteractiveServer</code> or
        <code>InteractiveAuto</code> in place of the one below; in a standalone Blazor WebAssembly app or a
        Blazor Hybrid one leave the <code>@@rendermode</code> line out, since everything there is interactive
        already.
    </p>
    <pre class="bb-code"><span class="k">@@page</span> <span class="s">"/scan"</span>
<span class="k">@@rendermode</span> InteractiveWebAssembly

&lt;<span class="k">BbScanner</span> Formats=<span class="s">"BbBarcodeFormat.QrCode"</span>
           Region=<span class="s">"BbScanRegion.CenteredSquare(0.7)"</span>
           OnScan=<span class="s">"HandleScan"</span>
           OnError=<span class="s">"HandleError"</span> /&gt;

<span class="k">@@if</span> (_error <span class="k">is not null</span>)
{
    &lt;<span class="k">p</span> role=<span class="s">"alert"</span>&gt;@@_error.Message&lt;/<span class="k">p</span>&gt;
}

<span class="k">@@code</span> {
    <span class="k">private</span> BbScannerException? _error;

    <span class="k">private void</span> HandleScan(BbBarcodeResult result)
    {
        _error = <span class="k">null</span>;
        Console.WriteLine(<span class="s">$"{result.Format}: {result.Text}"</span>);
    }

    <span class="k">private void</span> HandleError(BbScannerException error) =&gt; _error = error;
}</pre>

    <p class="bb-card-desc">
        Everything below comes with the component and needs no code: the status bar (top), a dimmed surround and a
        corner reticle marking the scan region, an outline drawn round each symbol it reads, and a control bar
        with <em>Pause</em>, <em>Stop</em>/<em>Start</em> and <em>Scan image</em> - plus a camera picker, a torch
        button and a zoom slider, each only when the device offers one. Drop an image file onto the scanner to scan
        it without the camera.
    </p>

    <div class="bb-demo">
        <div class="bb-demo-bar">
            <span class="bb-demo-label">Live</span>
            <span class="bb-soft">Needs HTTPS or localhost, and camera permission</span>
        </div>
        <div class="scan-sample">
            <div class="scan-layout">
                <div class="scan-view">
                    <BbScanner Formats="BbBarcodeFormat.All"
                               Region="BbScanRegion.CenteredSquare(0.75)"
                               OnScan="OnScan" />
                </div>
                <div class="scan-panels">
                    <section class="scan-panel">
                        <h4 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-success"></span> Last scan</h4>
                        @if (_last is not null)
                        {
                            <dl class="bb-params">
                                <dt>Format</dt>
                                <dd><span class="bb-badge">@_last.Format</span></dd>
                                <dt>Text</dt>
                                <dd>@_last.Text</dd>
                                <dt>Raw bytes</dt>
                                <dd>@_last.RawBytes.Length</dd>
                                <dt>Position</dt>
                                <dd>@_last.BoundingBox in a @_last.FrameWidth×@_last.FrameHeight frame</dd>
                            </dl>
                        }
                        else
                        {
                            <div class="bb-empty">Scan something to see the strongly typed result here.</div>
                        }
                        <p class="bb-card-desc">
                            No camera to hand? Use <a href="image">image scanning</a>, or open the
                            <a href="samples">sample barcodes</a> on another screen.
                        </p>
                    </section>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-warning"></span> Where it runs</h3>
    <p class="bb-card-desc">
        The camera is always opened by the page - the browser, or the WebView of a Blazor Hybrid app - and only
        in a secure context: a page served over HTTPS, or from <code>localhost</code>. What the hosting model
        decides is where .NET runs, and so where the frames are decoded.
    </p>
    <div class="bb-scroll">
        <table class="bb-table">
            <thead>
                <tr><th>Hosting model</th><th>What to do</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td class="bb-table-key">Blazor WebAssembly</td><td>Nothing else: put <code>&lt;BbScanner /&gt;</code> on a page. The fastest path - each frame crosses into managed memory once, through a <code>[JSImport]</code> memory view, and is decoded in the browser.</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="bb-table-key">Blazor Web App</td><td>Put the package in the project the scanning pages render in: the .Client project for <code>InteractiveWebAssembly</code> and <code>InteractiveAuto</code>, the server project for <code>InteractiveServer</code>. Under Auto the first visit scans on the circuit and later ones in the browser; no page needs <code>@@rendermode InteractiveWebAssembly</code> of its own.</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="bb-table-key">Blazor Server</td><td>The frame loop and the decoder run on the server. The page sends only the luminance of each frame's searched region - about 63 KB for a 640×360 frame through <code>CenteredSquare(0.7)</code>, against about 900 KB of RGBA - in slices under SignalR's 32 KB message limit, so the limit can stay as it is. Set a <code>Region</code>, and on a slow link lower <code>TargetFrameRate</code> and <code>MaxProcessingSize</code>. If the circuit is gone for 20 seconds, the page closes the camera.</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="bb-table-key">Blazor Hybrid</td><td>In a .NET MAUI app, <code>Bit.BbScanner.Maui</code> opens the device's own camera - CameraX, AVFoundation, MediaCapture - and its frames go straight to the decoder in the app; <code>builder.UseBbScanner()</code>, the BlazorWebView wrapped in a <code>BbScannerHost</code>, which draws the preview behind it, and the camera permission on each platform: <code>CAMERA</code> on Android, <code>NSCameraUsageDescription</code> on iOS and Mac Catalyst, the <code>webcam</code> capability on Windows. In WPF and Windows Forms the scanner opens WebView2's camera.</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="bb-card" id="lazy-loading">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-primary"></span> Lazy loading on WebAssembly</h3>
    <p class="bb-card-desc">
        There is nothing to register, so a WebAssembly app can fetch BbScanner only when someone opens a page
        with a scanner on it. Mark the assembly as lazy loaded in the project that runs in the browser, and load
        it before that page first renders:
    </p>
    <pre class="bb-code">&lt;<span class="k">ItemGroup</span>&gt;
  &lt;<span class="k">BlazorWebAssemblyLazyLoad</span> Include=<span class="s">"Bit.BbScanner.wasm"</span> /&gt;
&lt;/<span class="k">ItemGroup</span>&gt;</pre>
    <pre class="bb-code">@@* App.razor: the router waits for OnNavigateAsync before it renders the page. *@@
<span class="k">@@using</span> Microsoft.AspNetCore.Components.WebAssembly.Services
<span class="k">@@inject</span> LazyAssemblyLoader LazyAssemblyLoader

&lt;<span class="k">Router</span> AppAssembly=<span class="s">"typeof(App).Assembly"</span> OnNavigateAsync=<span class="s">"OnNavigateAsync"</span>&gt;
    ...
&lt;/<span class="k">Router</span>&gt;

<span class="k">@@code</span> {
    <span class="k">private async</span> Task OnNavigateAsync(NavigationContext context)
    {
        <span class="k">if</span> (context.Path == <span class="s">"scan"</span>)
            <span class="k">await</span> LazyAssemblyLoader.LoadAssembliesAsync([<span class="s">"Bit.BbScanner.wasm"</span>]);
    }
}</pre>
    <p class="bb-card-desc">
        The stylesheet is still injected at startup, since the package's Blazor initializer does not wait for the
        assembly, and the component imports its JavaScript module the first time it renders. Only the pages
        loaded after the assembly may use BbScanner's types - not a layout, a component on every page or
        <code>Program.cs</code>. Do not lazy load in an AOT build: AOT compiles every assembly into
        <code>dotnet.native.wasm</code>, so there is nothing left to download later, and lazy loading an
        AOT-compiled assembly can fail at startup. Since camera scanning wants AOT, lazy loading suits an app that
        reads still images or scans rarely.
    </p>
</div>

<div class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-primary"></span> How it works</h3>
    <p class="bb-card-desc">
        The browser opens the camera and copies pixels; every stage after that is C#, and reuses its buffers
        from frame to frame.
    </p>
    <div class="bb-scroll">
        <table class="bb-table">
            <thead>
                <tr><th>Stage</th><th>Where</th><th>What happens</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td class="bb-table-key">Capture</td><td>Browser</td><td><code>getUserMedia</code> feeds a <code>&lt;video&gt;</code>; a canvas scales frames to the processing size.</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="bb-table-key">Transfer</td><td>Boundary</td><td>On WebAssembly, one <code>JSImport</code> call copies RGBA into a reused managed buffer. Under Server and Hybrid the page sends the searched region's luminance instead, in slices of 30 KB.</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="bb-table-key">Luminance</td><td>C#</td><td>Fixed point BT.601 weights, with optional subsampling in the same pass.</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="bb-table-key">Binarise</td><td>C#</td><td>Adaptive 8×8 block thresholds for matrix codes, per-row histograms for linear ones.</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="bb-table-key">Detect</td><td>C#</td><td>Finder pattern search, perspective transform and grid sampling.</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="bb-table-key">Decode</td><td>C#</td><td>Reed-Solomon correction, de-interleaving and bit stream parsing.</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-success"></span> Where to go next</h3>
